</ul><h1>Cleaning Time</h1><p>There are limits to logic.</p>
<p>Take, for example, the winter. The temperatures drop. The sun goes down earlier. The weather can get scary. Logically speaking, this means you will spend more time at home. It’s not that you’ll go full-on hibernating-bear mode, but the reduction of options and increased effort to leave will result in more hunkering down.</p>
<p>So far, logic holds strong.</p>
<p>Here’s where things get complicated, though. The increased amount of time you spend at home will also increase your desire for said home to be clean. And the openness of your schedule should accommodate this. Logically speaking, it should be easier to tidy up these circumstances, given that you have the hours and will reap the rewards to a far greater extent than in other seasons, when your home is just a place to crash between adventures.</p>
<p>But it isn’t easier.</p>
<p>If anything, it’s harder.</p>
<p>Somehow, fitting a weekly cleaning session into a busy Saturday afternoon in May often feels way more doable than taking a leisurely Sunday morning in January to vacuum with no plans looming over you.</p>
<p>At least, this is how it goes for me.</p>
